          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            In the 1920s homology and cohomology were known for simplicial complexes and there were attempts to extend the definitions to first of all compact metric spaces and then more general spaces.
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Leopold+Vietoris"} Leopold Vietoris
            (1927) came up with a construction and then shortly after Alexandrov and Čech gave a different one involving
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+nerve"} the nerve that now bears Čech's name
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and an open cover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            It was noted that all the calculations of Vietoris homology gave the same answer as Čech homology. In 1952, C. H. Dowker showed why. His
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dowker%27s+theorem"} result
            is a beautiful mix of abstraction and concrete explicit calculation (and is not that well known unfortunately).
          %p First some abstraction:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                We take the set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                and the open cover
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒰
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi x
                        %mo ∈
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\in U
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
                will be considered to be
                %em related to
                the set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
                in the cover
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi 𝒰
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            %li
              %p
                We replace this by an abstract setting of a set
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
                and a relation
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mo ⊆
                        %mi X
                        %mo ×
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R\subseteq X\times Y
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Y
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
          .query
            %p
              Thinks: does what follows have a good generalisation to
              = succeed "?" do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spans"} spans
          %h2#definition Definition:
          %p
            The
            %strong Vietoris complex
            of a relation
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+complex"} simplicial complex
            specified by
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Vertex set: the set of vertices is the set
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            %li
              %p
                Simplices : a
                = succeed "-simplex" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi p
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi K
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
                is a set
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi p
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                      %mo ⊆
                      %mi X
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x_0, \ldots, x_p\}  \subseteq X
                such that there is some
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y \in Y
                with
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi x
                        %mi i
                      %mi R
                      %mi y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_i R y
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mi …
                      %mo ,
                      %mi p
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i  = 0, 1, \ldots, p
          %p
            The Vietoris complex of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            will be denoted
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V(R)
          %p
            The special, and original, case where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            comes from an open cover
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            will be denoted
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi 𝒰
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V(X,\mathcal{U})
            or simply
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒰
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V(\mathcal{U})
            and will be called the
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                Vietoris complex of the open covering
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi 𝒰
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
          %h2#remarks Remarks
          %p
            In fact each relation will give
            %strong two
            simplicial complexes, one as above, the other obtained by reversing the roles of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            in the above. In other words, in the usual way define the
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/opposite+relation"} opposite relation
            by ‘
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mi op
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi Y
                  %mo ×
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R^{op}\subseteq Y\times X
            is the relation in which
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi yR
                    %mi op
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} yR^{op}x
            if and only if
            = succeed "\u2019." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi xRy
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} xRy
          %p
            We define
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi R
                    %mi op
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(R) = V(R^{op})
            and call it the
            %strong Čech nerve
            or
            %strong Čech complex
            of the relation
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          %p
            If we look at this in the case of the relation coming from a space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and an open covers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            you get exactly the nerve of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒰
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{U}
            as discussed in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%C4%8Cech+methods"} Čech methods
          %p
            There are natural questions that arise here. One is how to turn these
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+complexes"} simplicial complexes
            into
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+sets"} simplicial sets
            %em one
            reply is take a total order (or at least pick a partial order that any simplex is ordered in that partial order; another reply would be to take each
            = succeed "-simplex" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-times," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo !
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (n+1)!
          %p
            Another obvious question is to compare
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V(R)
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(R)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dowker%27s+Theorem"} Dowker's Theorem
          %p A final question (and one I can answer!) is: are there useful applications of this construction other than in (co)homology. The answer is most decidedly ‘yes’.
          %h2#volodin_spaces Volodin spaces
          %p
            One of the ways of constructing the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory"} algebraic K-theory
            of a ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is to construct a simplicial set / complex from the stable
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/general+linear+group"} general linear group
            and the family of cosets of subgroups of upper triangular matrices. (This method is due to
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/I.A.+Volodin"}> I.A. Volodin
              \.)
          %p
            The method can be applied to other groups with given families of subgroups as is described at
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Volodin+spaces"} Volodin spaces
          %h2#higher_generation_by_subgroups Higher generation by subgroups
          %p
            It is notable that the same situation but with corresponding Čech complex is used by Abels and Holz, (1993) for the calculation of syzygies and is applied to various settings with linear groups. They give induction methods for homological finiteness criteria for the groups. This is discussed in more detail in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+generation+by+subgroups"} higher generation by subgroups
          %h2#hidden_stasheff_polytopes__ ‘Hidden Stasheff polytopes … ‘
          %p Kapranov and Saito have studied the syzygies of the usual presentation of the Steinberg group. These are expected to give a combinatorial construction of the classifying space for the algebraic K-theory of rings. They use Volodin’s construction but their combinatorial construction would seem to be related to that of Abels and Holz. They conjecture various results in this. but there is still some mystery about what results have been proved, beyond those published in their paper.
          %h2#gavin_wraiths_puzzle Gavin Wraith’s puzzle
          %p
            Gavin Wraith posted a puzzle on the
            = succeed "Caf\u00E9" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V(R)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(R)
            are homotopy equivalent:
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "http://golem.ph.utexas.edu/category/2009/07/a_puzzle_from_gavin_wraith.html"} A Puzzle From Gavin Wraith
          %p
            Perhaps this fact is
            = succeed "?" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dowker%27s+Theorem"} Dowker's Theorem
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homotopy"} persistent homotopy
          %h2#references References
          %p
            Discussion in
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/persistent+homotopy+theory"} persistent homotopy theory
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J.+F.+Jardine"}> J. F. Jardine
              ,
              %em Data and homotopy types
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1908.06323"} arXiv:1908.06323
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ]
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Wikipedia,
                %em
                  %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Vietoris–Rips_complex"} Vietoris-Rips complex
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/I.+Volodin"}> I. Volodin
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Algebraic K-theory as extraordinary homology theory on the category of associative rings with unity
              %p No. 4, 859-887).
            %li
              %p
                H. Abels and S. Holz,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Higher generation by subgroups
            %li
              %p M. Kapranov and M. Saito, 1999, Hidden Stasheff polytopes in algebraic K-theory and in the space of Morse functions , in Higher homotopy structure in topology and mathematical physics (Poughkeepsie, N.Y. 1996) , volume 227 of Contemporary Mathematics , 191–225, AMS.
